.policy "clippy-needless-raw-string-hashes-one-allowed" revision 2
.description "Use no more hashes on a Rust raw string literal than its contents need; this variant always allows a single hash."
.language rust
.mode steer
.scope include "**/*"

#include "rust/syntax-literals.rr"
//   raw_str(n, hashes, body)   a raw string literal's `#` count and contents
//   hashes_needed(n, k)        the `#`s its contents need, as Clippy counts them

// Clippy's needless_raw_string_hashes (pedantic) under `allow-one-hash-in-raw-strings = true`: one `#` is
// always allowed, and more than the contents need is reported.
//   r##"\d"##   r##"a "b" c"##   reported        r#"\d"#   r##"a"#b"##   not reported
// Clippy leaves a raw string with neither `\` nor `"` (`r#"x"#`) to needless_raw_strings when that lint is
// enabled too, which the recording has; with it off, needless_raw_string_hashes reports `r#"x"#` itself.
// Lint levels are not facts here, so the rule takes both as enabled (NOTES-rust.md (f)).
.violation needless_raw_string_hashes(at: node) code "needless_raw_string_hashes"
    "Remove the extra # marks from this raw string, keeping one or as many as its contents need to close it."
    suppress clippy "clippy::needless_raw_string_hashes", "clippy::pedantic", "warnings"
needless_raw_string_hashes(n) :- raw_str(n, k, b), (contains("\\", b) ; contains("\"", b)),
    hashes_needed(n, j), j < k, k > 1.
